            <ArticleTitle>A 9-year-old boy with atypical retroauricular pain: a case report.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>718-9</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <ELocationID EIdType="doi" ValidYN="Y">10.1016/j.amjoto.2013.06.003</ELocationID>
            <ELocationID EIdType="pii" ValidYN="Y">S0196-0709(13)00151-8</ELocationID>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText>Idiopathic causes of retroauricular pain are rarely seen in clinical practice. We present a 9-year-old child who suffered from atypical retroauricular pain resistant to conventional treatment. After excluding any other cause of retroauricular pain, a nerve block was performed with a 0.3 ml lidocaine 1% injection into the trigger point. We believe that this case report is important because in the literature there are no similar cases described in children.</AbstractText>
